Keyweave does not stamp a blanket SPDX identifier onto built artifacts: the bundle carries
Apache-2.0 material alongside MIT, and a single MIT banner over both would misstate the
license of the decoder.

LICENSES/ holds the full text of every license the built bundle is under, one file per
package, copied verbatim from the package as installed. The rule that produced that
directory is the zxing-cpp argument above applied to the MIT dependencies rather than only
to the Apache one: MIT requires its permission notice and copyright line to accompany the
software, and a package that ships a LICENSE file into node_modules has not shipped it to
anyone we later hand a bundle to. One deliberate exception: decimen-optical-transfer is not
duplicated here, because its LICENSE is already preserved verbatim in this tree at
client/vendor/decimen/LICENSE, which the entry above says and a reader can check.

WHERE THESE TEXTS LIVE IN A DEPLOYMENT, AND IT IS NOT INSIDE THE BUNDLE. NOTICE and
LICENSES/ are uploaded ALONGSIDE client/dist rather than emitted into it. The reason is a
collision between two rules that are both right: client/test/build-no-external-origin.test.ts
scans every emitted byte for an http(s) origin and permits none in a default build (residual
R13), and license texts carry URLs. Measured, by emitting both paths into a scratch build
and running the gate's own scan over it: thirteen origins, from apache.org, paulmillr.com,
opensource.org and the github.com links in this file. Neither side can give way honestly -
the license text is verbatim by obligation, and an origin scan with an exemption list is a
weaker wall than one without - so the legal text is served next to the bundle instead. The
consequence worth stating: these files are NOT covered by the artifact hashes a release
publishes, which cover client/dist only. docs/DEPLOY.md step 5 carries the upload.
